---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { contactEmail } from '../site';

/**
 * The privacy policy, and the URL the App Store asks for.
 *
 * Every claim here is a claim about the app as built: no network calls, no analytics, no
 * account, storage in AsyncStorage, the PIN in the device keychain. If any of that changes in
 * the app, it has to change here on the same day — a privacy page that has drifted is worse
 * than no privacy page.
 *
 * TODO before launch: Faisal to read this end to end, and put a real address in `site.ts`.
 */
const updated = '24 September 2026';

const nots = [
  'No account, no sign-in, no profile on anybody\'s server.',
  'No analytics, no usage statistics, no crash reporting.',
  'No advertising identifiers, no ad networks, no trackers.',
  'No third party receives anything about you, because nothing is sent to one.',
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="Wealth-it has no account, no server and no analytics. Everything it knows about your money is stored on your phone. This is the whole policy."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">Nothing leaves your phone.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--s-block);">
        This is the entire policy, and it is short because the app does very little that a
        policy needs to explain. Last updated {updated}.
      </p>

      <ul class="ticks" style="margin-top: var(--s-section);">
        {nots.map(n => <li><Icon name="check" /><span>{n}</span></li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--ruled">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose">
      <h2>What the app stores</h2>
      <p>
        Wealth-it keeps a ledger: your wallets and their opening balances, the transactions you
        record, the categories and labels you make, your budgets, your scheduled payments, your
        settings, and a profile name and picture if you set one.
      </p>
      <p>
        All of it is written to storage belonging to the app, on your phone. It is covered by
        whatever protects the rest of your phone — the lock screen, the device encryption, and
        the sandbox the operating system puts around every app. If you have set a PIN for
        Wealth-it, the PIN is held in the device keychain, never in the ledger and never in a
        backup file.
      </p>

      <h2>What the app sends</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. There is no server in this product. The app makes no network requests of its
        own, so there is no telemetry to switch off, no consent to give and nothing to opt out
        of. Nobody — including whoever wrote it — knows that you installed it, opened it, or
        recorded a single dirham.
      </p>
      <p class="mu">
        Which also means there is nothing to restore from if the phone is lost. That trade is
        deliberate, and it is the reason the app keeps offering to make you a backup.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions, and why each one is asked for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Photos</strong> — only if you choose a profile picture. The image you pick is
          copied into the app and stays on the phone. The app does not read your library
          otherwise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Face ID, Touch ID or the Android equivalent</strong> — only if you turn on the
          app lock. The check happens on the device; the app is told yes or no and never sees
          anything biometric.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Notifications</strong> — only if you turn reminders on. They are scheduled on
          the phone, by the phone. There are no push notifications, because pushing one would
          need a server to push it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Files</strong> — when you export a backup or a CSV, or pick a backup to
          restore. The app writes the file where you tell it to and does nothing else with it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Refuse any of these and the rest of the app carries on. If the phone refuses a
        permission, the screen says so rather than pretending the thing worked.
      </p>

      <h2>Backups and exports</h2>
      <p>
        A backup is a file. Once you have saved or shared it, where it goes is up to you and
        subject to whatever holds it — your files app, your cloud drive, the message you sent it
        in. The app has no further say in the matter, and no copy of its own. A backup does not
        contain your PIN.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting everything</h2>
      <p>
        Delete the app. That removes the ledger with it, and there is no second copy anywhere to
        chase down, no account to close and nobody to email about it. Any backup files you saved
        yourself are still wherever you put them.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Wealth-it is a personal ledger with no accounts, no messaging, no content from anyone
        else and no data collection. It is not directed at children, and it collects nothing from
        anybody of any age.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        The page you are reading is a static file. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics, embeds
        nothing from anywhere else, and loads its own fonts from its own server. There is no
        banner asking your consent for any of that because there is nothing to consent to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Being honest about the one exception: whoever hosts these files can see the ordinary
        request logs any web server keeps — an IP address, a page, a time. That is the web
        working as designed, it is not something the site does on purpose, and it is the only
        record of your visit that exists anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If the app ever starts doing something this page does not describe, this page changes
        first, and the date at the top changes with it.
      </p>

      <h2>Asking about any of this</h2>
      <p>
        Write to <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>. There is no account to
        look up at the other end, so say what you need in the message.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .ticks { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--s-snug); }
  .ticks li { display: flex; gap: var(--s-snug); align-items: flex-start; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .ticks :global(svg) { width: 20px; height: 20px; flex: none; margin-top: 3px; color: var(--acc-t); }
</style>
